**Linear Search Algorithm:**

**Complete Implementation:**
```java
public class LinearSearch {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {10, 25, 30, 45, 50, 60, 75, 80};
        int target = 50;

        boolean found = false;
        int index = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
            if (numbers[i] == target) {
                found = true;
                index = i;
                break;  // ✅ Important: stop searching
            }
        }

        if (found) {
            System.out.println(target + " found at index " + index);
        } else {
            System.out.println(target + " not found");
        }
    }
}
```

**Why Use break?**

**Without break (inefficient):**
```java
// ❌ Continues searching even after found
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] == target) {
        found = true;
        index = i;
        // Keeps looping unnecessarily!
    }
}
```

**With break (efficient):**
```java
// ✅ Stops immediately when found
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (arr[i] == target) {
        found = true;
        index = i;
        break;  // Exit loop early
    }
}
```

**Search Method:**
```java
public static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == target) {
            return i;  // Return index directly
        }
    }
    return -1;  // Not found
}

// Usage
int[] numbers = {10, 25, 30, 45, 50};
int index = linearSearch(numbers, 30);

if (index != -1) {
    System.out.println("Found at index " + index);
} else {
    System.out.println("Not found");
}
```

**Finding All Occurrences:**
```java
public static void findAll(int[] arr, int target) {
    boolean found = false;

    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == target) {
            System.out.println("Found at index " + i);
            found = true;
            // NO break - continue searching
        }
