**Array Copying - Shallow vs Deep:**

**Shallow Copy (WRONG):**
```java
// ❌ Assignment copies reference, not array
int[] original = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int[] copy = original;  // Points to SAME array!

copy[0] = 100;  // Modifies original too!

System.out.println(original[0]);  // 100 (changed!)
System.out.println(copy[0]);      // 100 (same array)
```

**Deep Copy (CORRECT):**

**Method 1: Manual Loop**
```java
int[] original = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int[] copy = new int[original.length];

for (int i = 0; i < original.length; i++) {
    copy[i] = original[i];
}

copy[0] = 100;  // Only affects copy

System.out.println(original[0]);  // 1 (unchanged)
System.out.println(copy[0]);      // 100 (modified)
```

**Method 2: Using clone()**
```java
int[] original = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int[] copy = original.clone();

copy[0] = 100;  // Independent copy

System.out.println(original[0]);  // 1 (unchanged)
System.out.println(copy[0]);      // 100 (modified)
```

**Why Shallow Copy Fails:**
```java
int[] arr1 = {1, 2, 3};
int[] arr2 = arr1;  // Both variables point to same array

// Memory view:
// arr1 → [1, 2, 3] ← arr2 (same array object)

// Changing either affects both:
arr1[0] = 10;
System.out.println(arr2[0]);  // 10 (same array!)
```

**Deep Copy Creates New Array:**
```java
int[] arr1 = {1, 2, 3};
int[] arr2 = arr1.clone();

// Memory view:
// arr1 → [1, 2, 3]
// arr2 → [1, 2, 3] (different array object)

// Independent modifications:
arr1[0] = 10;
System.out.println(arr1[0]);  // 10
System.out.println(arr2[0]);  // 1 (unchanged)
```

**All Deep Copy Methods:**

**1. Manual Loop:**
```java
int[] copy = new int[original.length];
for (int i = 0; i < original.length; i++) {
    copy[i] = original[i];
}
```

**2. clone() Method:**
```java
int[] copy = original.clone();
```

