int[] numbers;
numbers[0] = 10;  // NullPointerException!

// ✅ CORRECT - Allocate memory first
int[] numbers = new int[5];
numbers[0] = 10;  // Safe
```

**4. Shallow Copy Instead of Deep Copy:**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Copies reference
int[] original = {1, 2, 3};
int[] copy = original;
copy[0] = 100;  // Modifies original too!

// ✅ CORRECT - Create new array
int[] copy = original.clone();
copy[0] = 100;  // Only affects copy
```

**5. Wrong Index Calculation in Reverse:**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - Off by one
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length / 2; i++) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[arr.length - i];  // Wrong formula!
    arr[arr.length - i] = temp;    // Crashes!
}

// ✅ CORRECT - Use length-1-i
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length / 2; i++) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[arr.length - 1 - i];  // Correct
    arr[arr.length - 1 - i] = temp;
}
```

**6. Modifying Array in for-each:**
```java
// ❌ WRONG - for-each doesn't modify array
int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
for (int num : arr) {
    num = num * 2;  // Only modifies copy!
}
// arr is still {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}

// ✅ CORRECT - Use indexed loop
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    arr[i] = arr[i] * 2;  // Modifies array
}
```

